Maintained resource metadata lifecycle — implementation plan
==========================================================

Source proposal: [Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Proposal.md](Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Proposal.md).
Parent integration gate: [Rendering.GpuCommands.todo](Rendering.GpuCommands.todo), subphase 8i.
Initial inventory: [Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Inventory.md](Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Inventory.md).
Status: Phases 1–4 complete; Phases 5–7 remain pending.

Goal
  Maintain canonical resource facts from observed creation, guaranteed defaults and subsequent commands.
  Covered resource consumers reuse those facts without descriptor discovery. Every remaining native read
  has an explicit cause and concrete consumer. Preserve native behavior, original identities, dependency
  retirement, existing RHI ownership and binding suppression.

Controlling contracts
  The source proposal governs this redesign, including its narrowed adoption/import query policy.
  [Rendering.OpenTKBindingInterception.Proposal.md](Rendering.OpenTKBindingInterception.Proposal.md)
  governs native ABI, generated forwarding, saved-original bypass, callback lifetime and exception containment.
  [Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md](Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md) and
  [Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md](Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md) govern RHI boundaries and no-shim migration.
  [Rendering.GpuResources.md](Rendering.GpuResources.md) and
  [Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md](Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md) identify retained ownership, publication,
  preparation and consumer contracts; their existing acquisition behavior is revised by the source proposal.
  [Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Inventory.md](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Inventory.md),
  [Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.ApiCoverage.md](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.ApiCoverage.md) and
  [Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Analysis.md](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Analysis.md) locate existing
  families/readers and historical findings; they do not override the approved narrowed query policy.

Mandatory contract — applies to every implementation phase
  - Keep one GpuResourceManager registry and one OpenGlStateCache authority. Wrappers receive complete
    snapshots; no metadata groups, per-property metadata masks, requested/actual mirrors, parallel
    registry, wrapper-local tracking, new share-group registry or generic event/transaction framework.
  - Migrate creation/defaults, realization, mutations, readers, consumers, dependencies, failure/deletion
    handling and tests together. Remove superseded paths in that unit. No intermediate forwarding shim,
    compatibility facade, old-name alias or duplicate publication route. Pull blocking dependencies into
    the unit or merge units; later cleanup is not permission to leave a completed family incomplete.
  - Unmigrated families may retain their original implementation until their own complete migration;
    completed families cannot rely on those old paths for their claimed lifecycle or query guarantees.
  - Native callbacks forward original arguments once before observing, preserve outputs/errors and never
    veto, suppress, cancel, retry or throw into the external caller. Managed backend calls retain saved-original
    dispatch and their single private manager publication. No new enter/exit counters or uninstall waits.
  - Interpret native arguments beneath RHI; portable wrappers, schemas and manager consumers receive semantic
    facts. Use named enum members. Both GL roots and supported default/Core/Ext/Arb owners are in scope;
    legacy features and auxiliary owners remain excluded. Managed execution retains the OpenGL 4.5 baseline.
  - Classify represented relevance and proven rejection before interruption or availability withdrawal.
    Established request/default/derived facts update the same canonical entry. Uncertainty withdraws affected
    certification without fabricating success. No universal GetError drain, Is-object probing, verification
    read, retry or silent-allocation-failure detector. Use cached capabilities in native observations.
  - Borrowing/adoption of covered objects is query-free association. Initial discovery requires recorded
    pre-install provenance or an unknown identity encountered through a supported EXT named implicit-creation
    operation. Record the latter cause without assuming defaults or querying in callbacks; allow one initial
    read at explicit adoption, then require explicit recovery after failure. Ordinary binds/missing tracking
    or absence of a manager alone grant no discovery; local RHI initialization uses its supplied snapshot.
    Imports have no blanket query exemption.
  - Preserve explicit shader/compiler output, unsized-format resolution, cached sample resolution with the
    approved one-time fallback, documented targeted recovery and kind-specific deletion-outcome reads.
    Any additional import read must name the missing fact, why intercepted setup cannot supply it and its
    consumer. External-memory-backed textures have one approved immutable-storage flag read after a
    known-mutable allocation attempt; dimensions, format and levels remain supplied by the command.
    Semaphore import has one
    approved targeted IsSemaphore lifecycle outcome check at the RHI boundary for wait/signal admission;
    external import observers do not probe. Unimported owned reservations are disposable, not consumable.
  - Context identity remains manager-owned and context-affine; equal names in different contexts do not
    imply shared identity. Keep reserved/realized status separate from availability and terminal retirement.
    Do not claim shared-context execution or extend ownership through reference retention.
  - Delegate builds/tests. Use canonical shader-enabled Debug and ordinary Release configuration. No new
    benchmark, performance baseline, instrumented Release, game launch, automatic staging or commit.

Family completion evidence — inherited by Phases 2–6
  Each family records its exact native symbol/owner/ABI routes and before/after reader/consumer routes in
  the lifecycle inventory. Tests exercise actual native interception and RHI saved-original execution,
  with unexpected descriptor reads rejected. Verify defaults, delayed realization where applicable,
  aliases, known/rejected/uncertain effects, output pointer lifetime, original error preservation, exactly-once
  dispatch/registration/publication, stable suppression, failure cleanup, name reuse and context retirement.
  Legitimate driver-produced reads have separate assertions. Optional hardware gaps remain explicit;
  deterministic delivery tests do not establish unsupported hardware execution. Complete second review
  and independent stage audit before checking off a family; update permanent docs to implemented behavior.

Phase 1: Complete lifecycle coverage and migration dependencies
  Outcome: every supported resource lifecycle and current reader has a named migration destination.
  This phase is inventory/design reconciliation only; no production placeholders or compatibility APIs.

  ☑ Expand Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Inventory.md from its initial factory/deletion candidates using
    the exact compiler-referenced OpenTK assembly. Record both roots, eligible owner, native symbol,
    ABI/result/output shape, creation/realization behavior, mutation/deletion aliases and owning phase.
    Include glGen*/glCreate*, shader/program factories, shader-program combined factories, glFenceSync,
    extension imports/storage establishment and later binds/named commands that realize reserved names.
    Validate the assembly identity instead of assuming the prior artifact still matches the compiler.
  ☑ Resolve the inventory's optional sync-import and legacy/excluded candidates against existing supported
    operations and authoritative native specifications. Do not expand support merely because a symbol exists.
    Record excluded features explicitly, including legacy assembly programs/display lists/EXT vertex shaders.
  ☑ Map each existing metadata field and represented execution fact to supplied input, guaranteed default,
    derived value or driver-produced output. Name consumer requirements and cached acceptance conditions.
    Distinguish reserved names, realized empty objects and allocated storage for each kind; use explicit
    native defaults rather than publishing C# default structs or inferring semantics from Gen/Create spelling.
  ☑ Inventory every metadata reader, Acquire*/Recover*/Refresh* entry point and consumer, including local
    tools, image projections, geometry certification, prepared shader bindings and backing dependencies.
    Name each method explicitly with its final reuse/query/removal disposition and migration owner.
    Identify production callback/reentry paths requiring completion exclusion; synthetic tests alone do
    not justify retaining guards. Carry relevance-before-interruption requirements into each family.
  ☑ Establish initial-adoption provenance at the existing installation/context boundary. Specify how callers
    distinguish pre-installation discovery from post-installation tracking defects and explicit recovery,
    without adding a second identity registry or guessing from a missing entry. Define manager/context/hook
    activation ordering before covered creation, including startup, reload and context-local supplied
    initialization. Phase 2 implements or verifies this ordering and qualifies its runtime behavior.
  ☑ Record per-family accepted/rejected/uncertain outcomes and required read causes. Identify all dependencies
    crossing the proposed phase boundaries; move blocking caller/dependency work into the owning unit.
    No broad import-discovery allowance or generic uncertainty fallback can substitute for this mapping.
  ☑ Completion: every supported lifecycle, represented fact, reader and consumer has an explicit disposition;
    unresolved coverage/semantic questions are resolved before the affected family begins implementation.

  Verification: fresh compiler-reference PE probe and source reconciliation recorded in the lifecycle
    inventory and Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Bindings.tsv (430 unique symbols, exact owner/ABI/overload
    matches). Second review and independent stage audit passed for inventory/design coverage. Runtime
    implementation and native qualification remain with Phases 2–7; no production changes in Phase 1.

Phase 2: Establish registration lifecycle with memory objects and buffers
  Depends on Phase 1.
  Outcome: raw OpenTK and RHI buffers maintain canonical storage facts without routine readback,
    including the memory-object lifetimes required by external-memory buffer storage.

  ☑ Extend GpuResourceRegistration/GpuResourceMetadataEntry and GpuResourceManager registry/publication
    services with reserved-versus-realized identity while retaining complete-snapshot availability,
    original registration identity, revisions and terminal retirement. Exercise the final API with buffers
    in this unit; add no unused generic lifecycle scaffolding for later families.
  ☑ Implement or verify the manager/context/hook activation ordering defined in Phase 1. Ensure the existing
    manager can receive covered creation observations before the first explicit RHI operation, after context
    recreation and after binding reload. Preserve original-context ownership and native always-forward
    behavior; missing readiness must not silently lose creation facts or trigger later descriptor discovery.
    Add lifecycle tests that exercise those three entry conditions through the native interception boundary,
    asserting original-identity registration/default publication once and no compensating descriptor reads.
  ☑ Implement generated post-call buffer creation observers for all inventoried Gen/Create aliases and
    subsequent realization operations. Include the array Gen/Create/Bind identity prerequisites for
    BindVertexBuffer/VertexArrayVertexBuffer and EXT generic offset buffer realization; full VAO
    configuration certification remains in Phase 6. Consume returned arrays synchronously with exact native widths.
    Reject zero/detected failed results and invalid counts; do not replace an existing allocation from
    stale output values. Preserve original-context admission and initialization defaults.
  ☑ Migrate the prerequisite GpuMemoryObject/GpuMemoryObjectMetadata family completely: EXT creation,
    import, RHI handoff, native observations, consumers and deletion. Preserve empty metadata, native handle
    ownership, capability checks and original-context cleanup. Import establishes only its represented facts;
    no descriptor query follows merely from receiving a handle. Qualify failed imports, repeated setup and
    deletion/name reuse before buffer storage relies on those identities. Include all platform aliases in scope.
  ☑ Migrate GpuBufferObject, GpuVbo, GpuEbo, GpuUniformBuffer, GpuShaderStorageBuffer, GpuAtomicCounterBuffer,
    GpuIndirectBuffer, GpuPixelPackBuffer and GpuPixelUnpackBuffer through GpuBufferMetadata.
    Replace ObserveBufferStorage blanket withdrawal with supplied capacity/usage/storage-mode/flag effects
    for established commands. Cover mutable/immutable, named/bound and supported extension allocation forms.
    Mapping/activity stays execution-owned; role/content extent stays wrapper policy. Content uploads do
    not publish capacity or renew storage identity.
  ☑ Migrate GpuResourceManager.BufferAcquisition, relevant AcquireResource/AcquireWrapperMetadata callers,
    buffer readers and engine geometry consumers to query-free maintained facts or explicit justified adoption.
    Introduce final provenance checks in existing acquisition services and migrate affected callers directly.
    Preserve buffer-texture original dependencies, whole-buffer range semantics and retirement notifications;
    bring any required dependent caller changes into this unit without claiming the image family is complete.
  ☑ Integrate buffer creation with the RHI private handoff and saved-original bypass: one registration and
    publication, failed candidate cleanup against original identity, no external callback duplication.
    Remove obsolete buffer readback/invalidation paths and unnecessary completion machinery.
  ☑ Run inherited family qualification, including pre-install adoption, tracked raw alias reuse, generated
    names before binding, rejected immutable mutation, storage replacement, stale output/name reuse and
    no-manager supplied initialization. Reconcile buffer documentation and inventory dispositions.
  ☑ Completion: buffer lifecycle, dependencies and consumer closure use the final maintained-fact route;
    no automatic descriptor recovery disguises missing buffer interception.

  Verification: canonical shader-enabled Debug and ordinary Release builds passed; the focused suite
    passed 173/173 in each configuration with zero skips. Generator tests passed 35/35 per configuration.
    Second review and independent stage audit passed after linked-document reconciliation. Receipts:
    artifacts/metadata-lifecycle/phase2/*adoption-final* and generator Debug/Release receipts in that directory.
    Optional EXT slots unavailable on the test driver use deterministic callback-boundary stand-ins;
    hardware EXT execution, installed-game startup, appearance and performance are not qualified.

Phase 3: Maintain semaphore, query and synchronization identities
  Depends on Phase 2's exercised registration lifecycle.
  Outcome: the remaining synchronization/import identities are maintained through their full lifecycles.

  ☑ Migrate GpuQuery/GpuQueryMetadata and GpuFence/GpuSyncMetadata through all retained generation,
    target-specific creation, fence creation and supported sync-import routes resolved in Phase 1.
    Preserve pointer-width sync results and kind-specific delayed realization; query/wait results remain
    explicit driver outputs rather than canonical descriptor fields.
  ☑ Migrate GpuSemaphore/GpuSemaphoreMetadata through EXT generation/import and deletion. Retain its empty
    schema, capability requirements, native handle ownership, import lifetime and original-context cleanup.
    Do not infer import success solely from a handle or introduce descriptor queries because it is imported.
  ☑ Update RHI creation/import operations, native observers, acquisition/retirement consumers and tests in
    the same unit. Registration/default publication occurs once for both native and RHI paths; imports
    update their actual represented lifecycle effects without manufacturing a second allocation identity.
    Preserve resource facts supplied to later memory-backed storage operations.
  ☑ Remove obsolete identity/discovery paths, retaining legitimate query/wait and deletion-outcome reads.
    Qualify native return/output preservation, failed imports, borrowed external handles, repeated setup,
    name reuse and context loss. Record unavailable native capabilities separately from deterministic tests.
  ☑ Completion: synchronization and semaphore consumers use established original lifetimes;
    empty metadata remains empty and every retained query has a specific non-descriptor purpose or justification.

  Verification: canonical shader-enabled Debug and ordinary Release builds passed; the combined focused
    lifecycle suite passed 231/231 in each configuration with zero skips. Generator tests passed 35/35
    per configuration. Second source review and independent stage audit passed after resolving query
    activity uncertainty, external interval ownership, canonical reference admission and nonfatal import
    diagnostics. Receipts: artifacts/metadata-lifecycle/phase3/phase3-*-closed.trx, *-closed.log and
    generator Debug/Release receipts in that directory. The binding inventory now includes 435 symbols.
    Actual query/fence and supported empty semaphore/memory creation/disposal routes were exercised;
    external-producer FD/Win32 imports use deterministic native-boundary stand-ins and are not hardware
    interop qualification. Installed-game startup, appearance and performance remain unqualified.
    Phases 4–7 and the parent 8i integration gate remain open.

Phase 4: Migrate image storage, views and framebuffer consumers together
  Depends on Phases 2–3.
  Outcome: texture/renderbuffer creation and configuration supply aliases, views and framebuffer images
    from maintained canonical facts, including imported-memory-backed storage.

  ☑ Add all inventoried texture/renderbuffer/framebuffer creation and realization observations. Establish
    target and kind-specific defaults only when realized. Preserve zero/default framebuffer surface
    semantics, empty image allocations, reserved names and extension realization behavior.
    Record unknown EXT named realization identities as awaiting one explicit discovery, without guessed
    defaults, callback readback or repeated automatic retries. Tracked reservations still realize query-free;
    discovery resolves the actual texture target independently of a rejected command's target.
    Retain validated buffer-texture association mode/ranges and original backing privately until target
    discovery; never infer whole-buffer mode or narrow native-width ranges from integer getters.
    Engine menu textures outside framebuffer references need provenance only for actual managed adoption;
    ordinary unknown bindings cannot manufacture descriptors or authorize discovery.
  ☑ Migrate GpuTexture and its owned subclasses, BorrowedTexture, GpuTextureView, GpuBufferTexture and
    GpuBufferView using GpuTextureMetadata. Forward dimensions, formats, levels/layers/faces, storage mode
    and represented parameters from every relevant named/bound/explicit-unit/extension command.
    Replace ObserveTextureStorage2D/3D and equivalent argument-discarding paths; cover mutable definitions,
    immutable storage, compressed definitions, external-memory storage and base/max-level changes.
      For external-memory storage, read only the immutable-storage flag once when a known-mutable
      texture's opaque imported payload prevents establishing whether the allocation succeeded.
  ☑ Derive immutable mip geometry and mutable mip-generation effects from known base images. Preserve
    array/cube/3D shape semantics and unallocated images. Subimage writes and immutable mip regeneration
    preserve descriptor/storage revisions when they only change contents. Preserve prior facts on proven
    rejection; targeted uncertainty cannot become an unconditional full-image scan.
  ☑ Migrate GpuRenderbuffer/GpuRenderbufferMetadata and renderbuffer-backed texture associations. Maintain
    extent/format from commands; resolve unsized image formats only where necessary. Use cached target/format
    sample capabilities and the approved single sample-count fallback for renderbuffers and multisample
    textures. Never read unrelated descriptor fields to resolve these outputs.
  ☑ Preserve distinct texture-view registrations, source allocation/storage dependencies and view-relative
    image geometry. Maintain buffer-view offsets/ranges/whole-buffer convention and renderbuffer associations
    from observed setup. Source replacement never silently retargets borrowers/views. Deletion and storage
    changes invalidate the appropriate existing dependencies without a second metadata mirror.
  ☑ Migrate GpuFramebuffer, DynamicGpuFramebuffer, StaticGpuFramebuffer and GpuFramebufferAttachment using
    GpuFramebufferMetadata and projected GpuImageMetadata. Apply individual attachment/subresource and
    attachmentless-default changes, preserve attachment modes and split read/draw selectors, and keep
    routing in the existing cache. Project images from known backing entries without native rediscovery.
  ☑ Migrate texture/image/framebuffer acquisition, parameter/image refresh, generation, adoption, owner-image
    delivery and backing-dependency consumers together. Covered borrowing and reference/attachment validation
    perform no queries. Preserve explicit pre-install discovery, recorded EXT implicit-creation discovery
    at initial adoption and documented targeted recovery; remove
    superseded descriptor scans and interrupt/refresh machinery without leaving compatibility routes.
  ☑ Qualify the complete creation → storage → view/attachment → consumption → replacement/deletion paths
    through both GL roots and RHI. Test partial cubes, level/layer selection, content-only effects, aliases,
    backing reuse, invalid commands, uncertain outcomes and imported memory. Reject unexpected descriptor
    reads and separately assert the narrow format/sample reads. Reconcile image/attachment docs and inventory.
  ☑ Completion: all image family callers and dependencies use maintained facts; source identity and complete
    snapshot availability remain correct, with no deferred image-consumer migration or blanket import queries.

  Verification: canonical shader-enabled Debug and ordinary Release builds passed. Each configuration
    passed 698/698 combined GPU/engine/lifecycle tests and 35/35 generator tests, with zero failures/skips.
    Second source review and independent completion audit passed. Receipts: artifacts/metadata-lifecycle/phase4,
    build-*-mips-v5.log, phase4-mips-v5-*.trx and phase4-generator-v5-*.trx.
    Optional EXT/import paths include deterministic native stand-ins; external-producer hardware interop,
    installed-game startup, appearance and performance are not qualified. Phases 5–7 and parent 8i remain open.

Phase 5: Maintain shader, executable and pipeline metadata
  Depends on Phases 2–4.
  Outcome: native executable identity and ordinary interface assignments use maintained facts while
    compiler/linker-produced results retain explicit completion reads.

  ☐ Add creation observations for GpuShaderModule/GpuShaderMetadata, GpuProgramObject and GpuProgramLayout/
    GpuProgramMetadata, and GpuProgramPipeline/GpuProgramPipelineMetadata. Cover shader/program factories,
    combined shader-program creation, pipeline Gen/Create realization and supported aliases from the inventory.
    Establish fixed stage and fresh unlinked defaults from inputs; do not reflect a newly empty program.
  ☐ Integrate existing immediate/deferred compilation, linking and binary installation with original-entry
    identity and explicit result/interface completion. Do not assume return of a program name proves a linked
    executable. Preserve installed-generation invalidation, failed candidate cleanup and fixed shader refs.
  ☐ Patch represented sampler/image-unit and block assignments from established calls against existing
    canonical interface facts. Numeric uniforms and ignored locations remain content/no-op observations.
    Link-input declarations affect the next executable; ordinary assignments never trigger full reflection.
    Preserve unsupported/uncertain represented assignments without inventing complete usable metadata.
  ☐ Keep pipeline stage/selection knowledge with its existing execution owner and the canonical pipeline
    schema empty. Migrate native program adoption, GpuProgramInterface, GpuPreparedBindings and shader/PSO
    consumers to supplied snapshots or explicit driver-output completion. Remove obsolete acquisition and
    refresh routes; retain only production-required interruption guards and relevance-first ordering.
  ☐ Qualify raw and RHI creation, successful/failed/deferred link, binary replacement, unit/block updates,
    irrelevant uniforms, pipeline aliases, deletion-pending objects and name reuse. Assert no stage discovery
    after known creation, no full reflection after assignments and legitimate executable reads at completion.
    Reconcile shader/prepared-binding contracts and lifecycle inventory.
  ☐ Completion: maintained executable facts and explicit driver outputs have distinct final routes;
    no preparation fallback, duplicate publication or stage-layout mirror remains.

Phase 6: Maintain vertex-array, transform-feedback and sampler lifecycles
  Depends on Phases 2–5.
  Outcome: remaining empty-schema resource kinds have tracked identities and revisions without descriptor scans.

  ☐ Migrate GpuVao/GpuVertexArrayMetadata, GpuTransformFeedback/GpuTransformFeedbackMetadata and
    GpuSampler/GpuSamplerMetadata through all retained generation/creation/realization/deletion aliases.
    Preserve resource-specific guaranteed defaults without treating all Gen* calls as equivalent.
  ☐ Maintain represented configuration revisions and execution-owned vertex/element bindings, transform-
    feedback activity and existing sampler knowledge. Cover bound/named/indexed/multi-bind effects where
    applicable. Keep all three canonical schemas empty; add no full VAO, transform-feedback or sampler mirror.
  ☐ Migrate GpuVertexAttribBinding/geometry certification and other readers/consumers using existing authored
    descriptions, observed revision knowledge and original identities. Any retained configuration read must
    identify why available observed facts cannot establish the actual consumer requirement; an empty schema
    or an external wrapper alone is not justification. Pull missing producer effects into this unit.
  ☐ Reconcile managed creation with native callbacks, operation cleanup, deletion dependencies and local tools.
    Remove obsolete acquisition/invalidation paths and unnecessary interruption guards. Qualify both roots,
    raw/RHI aliases, binding suppression, invalid configuration, activity transitions, name/context reuse and
    explicitly justified remaining reads. Update inventory and relevant permanent docs.
  ☐ Completion: remaining kinds preserve empty canonical schemas and their existing execution owners;
    complete creation/configuration/consumer routes have no hidden routine discovery fallback.

Phase 7: Qualify complete lifecycle coverage and reconcile architecture
  Depends on Phases 1–6; this is combined qualification, not deferred per-family migration or cleanup.
  Outcome: every covered resource lifecycle and remaining query is accounted for with verified consumer behavior.

  ☐ Reconcile every factory/configuration/deletion/realization inventory row with actual generated callbacks,
    backend saved-original execution and manager/cache effects. Check arguments and data flow, not only
    attribute/symbol presence. Resolve omissions in their owning family before declaring combined completion.
  ☐ Resolve all migration TODO comments referencing Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.todo, removing each
    comment only after its named family replacement is implemented and verified.
  ☐ Revisit every named reader/acquisition/recovery and interruption method from Phase 1. Confirm its final
    removal or narrowly justified consumer route, including startup/reload, initial adoption and local tools.
    Verify absent entries under covered creation are diagnosed as tracking defects without descriptor recovery.
    Validate optional import exceptions individually; no unexplained read may inherit a general exemption.
  ☐ Run delegated canonical shader-enabled Debug and ordinary Release builds and combined focused lifecycle
    tests. Cover raw OpenTK/engine-style and RHI creation, reserved realization, aliases/views/dependencies,
    imported setup, failed commands, pre-install adoption, explicit recovery, context recreation and name reuse.
    Confirm once-only dispatch/registration/publication and unchanged native errors/outputs, suppression and
    zero unexpected descriptor reads. Update obsolete managed interception fixtures to the native boundary.
  ☐ Verify each retained output-read exception with its own test: compiler/linker/interface results,
    unsized format resolution, cached versus fallback sample counts, documented recovery and deletion outcomes.
    Keep unsupported hardware execution and inherited unrelated suite failures explicit; focused checks do
    not establish package identity, live appearance or integrated rendering acceptance.
  ☐ Reconcile the source proposal, RHI contracts, lifecycle inventory, resource inventory/API coverage,
    acquisition analysis and permanent resource/shader-binding docs with implemented ownership and query rules.
    Record concise qualification receipts in the existing planning companions; do not create an extra audit report.
  ☐ Remove temporary migration documents after reconciling their coverage with the implemented system and
    automated checks, explicitly including docs/Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Bindings.tsv. Review other
    temporary inventories and planning evidence for removal; preserve any still-needed architectural
    decisions in the appropriate permanent documentation. Update all affected links and references,
    including this plan and Rendering.MetadataLifecycle.Inventory.md, so no active contract depends on
    a deleted artifact. Do not retain a parallel manually maintained binding specification.
  ☐ Perform second source review and independent completion audit against every proposal requirement and
    linked contract. Resolve required discrepancies before checking completion or the parent 8i gate.
    Parent RHI 8j/8k and integrated/package/user qualification in Phase 9 remain separate and unchecked.
  ☐ Completion: the full supported creation/configuration lifecycle maintains canonical facts; ordinary
    consumers perform no discovery, remaining reads have explicit evidence, and no migration shim or second
    authority remains. Hardware limitations are recorded without being represented as executed coverage.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Every source-proposal requirement maps to implemented behavior and appropriate evidence.
  ☐ All seven phases and inherited family completion requirements are satisfied, including original
    identities, reserved realization, complete snapshots, dependency retirement and context ownership.
  ☐ Covered borrowing/adoption is query-free; pre-install discovery, recorded EXT implicit-creation initial
    discovery and each remaining output/recovery read have explicit provenance and consumer justification.
    No blanket import/local-tool exception remains.
  ☐ No superseded readback/invalidation route, temporary shim, duplicate cache/registry or backend leak
    survives in a completed family. Documentation and the parent integration gate agree with actual evidence.

Open evidence and scope checks
  - Phase 1 resolves lifecycle coverage and excludes unsupported CL/X11 sync imports explicitly.
    Optional native hardware coverage still depends on the available test environment.
  - No imported-descriptor query exception is currently established. Any newly demonstrated need must meet
    the source proposal's exact-fact/setup/consumer test; it cannot become a general fallback policy.
  - Native observational publication retains the approved limitation on detecting silent allocation failure.
    This plan does not claim a universal success detector or introduce verification reads to approximate one.

Proposal coverage map
  One authority, complete snapshots, context identity and reserved realization: mandatory contract, Phases 1–2.
  Native creation results, once-only forwarding and backend bypass: inherited family evidence, Phases 2–6.
  Buffer facts and backing retirement: Phase 2, image dependency closure in Phase 4.
  Imported memory-object prerequisites: Phase 2; semaphore/query/sync identities and empty schemas: Phase 3.
  Texture/renderbuffer storage, views, image projection and framebuffer consumers: Phase 4.
  Shader/program/pipeline facts and driver-produced executable results: Phase 5.
  Vertex-array/transform-feedback/sampler empty schemas and execution knowledge: Phase 6.
  Adoption provenance, narrow reads, relevance/interruption and no-shim cleanup: mandatory contract,
    Phase 1 mapping, each owning family and Phase 7 cross-family verification.
  Documentation, combined validation, second review and independent completion audit: Phase 7.
